WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.060
What keeps a cell in the stem-cell state?

2
00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:06.940
Chapter one showed a pool that lasts while its clones come and go.

3
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.300
But the crypt base held only a few positions.

4
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.640
What limits them? Watch the same crypt, now with its signals drawn in.

5
00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:21.110
Start with the neighbourhood.

6
00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:26.070
A stem-cell niche is the local environment around stem cells.

7
00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:32.310
Its contact and signals bias whether a daughter keeps a stem-cell position or

8
00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:37.890
differentiates. In the mouse gut, Paneth cells in the lining and stromal cells

9
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.550
beneath it both contribute.

10
00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.690
Two kinds of signal run in opposite directions.

11
00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:49.840
Wnt is strongest at the crypt base and promotes self-renewal.

12
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.650
R-spondin, EGF and Notch also support the base.

13
00:00:55.070 --> 00:01:00.070
BMP is strongest higher up, among differentiated cells, and it suppresses

14
00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:01.070
self-renewal.

15
00:01:01.883 --> 00:01:07.463
During cell turnover, a blue stem cell is displaced upward, away from the strongest

16
00:01:07.463 --> 00:01:11.023
Wnt. What do you expect it to do?

17
00:01:16.517 --> 00:01:20.697
As cells move up, they become more likely to leave the stem-cell state.

18
00:01:22.057 --> 00:01:24.657
Less Wnt reaches them, and more BMP.

19
00:01:26.037 --> 00:01:28.697
The cell that remains at the base keeps its support.

20
00:01:29.977 --> 00:01:32.997
So the positions are limited because the support is local.

21
00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:35.650
Here is the key idea.

22
00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.690
In the mouse gut, being a stem cell looks less fixed.

23
00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.730
It looks more like a state that local signals support.

24
00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:48.670
Chapter one's clones competed for places inside that support.

25
00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:52.470
A winner holds a place, not a permanent title.

26
00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:55.793
A dish tests how much surroundings matter.

27
00:01:57.393 --> 00:02:02.753
Single sorted Lgr5 cells grew into organoids with matrix and added factors.

28
00:02:04.193 --> 00:02:07.073
Organoids are crypt-villus structures grown in culture.

29
00:02:08.373 --> 00:02:10.013
They could be replated repeatedly.

30
00:02:11.313 --> 00:02:15.473
No stromal cells were present; the matrix and factors supplied a niche.

31
00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:20.127
If the state depends on support, a harder question follows.

32
00:02:21.547 --> 00:02:24.087
Could a cell that has already left the base come back?

33
00:02:25.347 --> 00:02:28.487
Injury tests that, when the usual stem cells are lost.

34
00:02:29.887 --> 00:02:34.587
Reprogramming pushes further, asking whether a specialized nucleus can be reset

35
00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:35.607
beyond its tissue.

36
00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:40.907
So when you ask who is a stem cell, look at the place as well as the cell.

37
00:02:42.367 --> 00:02:45.527
Local niche signals help hold a cell in the stem-cell state.

38
00:02:46.727 --> 00:02:49.567
Whether a cell that left can return is the next test.
